9
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Recently, Sapien Labs released a first-of-its-kind report in the world, which showed that for every earlier age of smartphone acquisition as kids, the mental well-being as adults was worse and worse.

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:31,000
In a trend that was profoundly more impactful for girls than it was for boys, though it was bad for both.

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Leading publications around the world have picked up this research, which showed the significance of the findings and the importance for society to take note.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:52,000
To talk more about this research and what it means for us, I'm delighted to be with Dr. Tara Thiagarajan, founder of Sapien Labs and the Global Mind Project,

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
and the Chief Architect of the report.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
What are the biggest takeaways for you?

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
So, fundamentally, as the younger you got your cell phone, the worse your mental well-being as a young adult.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And the dimension that is most impacted by the age of which you got a smartphone is the social self, which is our ability to relate to others and our sense of self.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:33,000
And at the negative end, in terms of what are the kind of things, the most negative things that, you know, having a smartphone early seems to be driving, are suicidal thoughts is one of the most significant.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
And feelings, a sense of being detached from reality and feelings of aggression towards others.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:49,000
These are really alarming findings. And this is also what I'm hearing from several parents who have had kids that had smartphones early.

20
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Now, you have three teenage children. What did you do when you saw this report?

21
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Yeah. You know, you have a moment of panic. I think that, you know, it's certainly something that I wish I'd known a decade ago.

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
I mean, my kids are teenagers between 14 and 19. And, you know, it's a hard challenge to solve as a parent.

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:23,000
I think one of the things that we did, you know, was really bring the kids together and say, hey, look at this data and, you know, tell me what you see in yourself as, you know, happening with the smartphone.

24
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:32,000
And I think, you know, they recognize it themselves. And I think it gave us an impetus as a family to say that, you know what, we need to put our phones down at nine o'clock.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:41,000
And I say we because it's not just I think the solution is not just, oh, you take it away from the kids and then now everything is solved.

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:49,000
It's not that. It's what do you do with the time that you would have been on the smartphone? That's fundamentally important.

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,000
It's not just something we have to impose on the kids. It's something that we as parents need to be conscious of because I think it's always been so easy.

28
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
You know, even when your kids are really young to give them the iPad when you're at dinner because it's like it's just easier.

29
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Calms them down. Calms them down. They're distracted. They're not getting in your conversation.

30
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:19,000
But, you know, that engagement in your conversation, being at the dinner table as a little kid and watching the conversation, those are all important aspects of developing your social self.

31
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:33,000
It's like you're trying to understand the dynamics of the conversation of the people around you, you know, learning how to understand, you know, people's intent and meaning and all of that.

32
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And so I think part of it is being conscious of it and saying that is something that we are actively enabling for our child and, you know, put the phone down and engage.

33
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Tara, thank you so much for being here. Thanks.

34
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
So this is a hard, hard problem, but it's not entirely insurmountable.

35
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Don't try and tackle this individually. Gather together in your local community with other parents and prevail upon your schooling systems, prevail upon your local law or policymakers and ask them to remove the use of smartphones from schools.

36
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
There are already some movements underway towards that.

37
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
And then also some governments have begun to pass legislation to do so.

38
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
And finally, be the change you seek in your kids and role model this behavior yourself.

39
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:35,000
You can take action and we all must if we are to see a better future for those that will follow after us.

